Global Privacy Control, now that Chromium ships it.

Chromium 152 implements GPC itself. blink::IsGlobalPrivacyControlEnabled() in
third_party/blink/common/global_privacy_control/global_privacy_control_util.cc
gates the Sec-GPC header at all four call sites this patch used to edit by
hand - browser_initiated_resource_request.cc, render_frame_impl.cc, and the
dedicated/shared and service worker fetch contexts - and ipc_utils.cc already
allowlists kSecGPC, so the bad-message kill this patch's header warned about
cannot happen any more either. The navigator.globalPrivacyControl attribute is
upstream too, behind the GlobalPrivacyControl runtime feature.

All of which is implied by one flag. GlobalPrivacyControlForce has no status in
runtime_enabled_features.json5, so it is off, and two things follow from
turning it on: GlobalPrivacyControl lists it in implied_by, so the DOM
attribute becomes true, and the generator gives a base::Feature whose default
is FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT when status is "stable" (see the schema comment
above base_feature_status in that file), so IsGlobalPrivacyControlEnabled()
becomes true and the header is sent.

So nine files of re-implementation become one line, and the parts most likely
to rot at a Chromium bump - a hand-edited allowlist in ipc_utils.cc, an IDL
attribute, four header-injection sites - go back to being upstream's problem.

The behaviour is unchanged: the signal is sent unconditionally, with no
per-site toggle, which is what Brave and DuckDuckGo do and what the CCPA
recognises.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/runtime_enabled_features.json5
@@ -3467,10 +3467,11 @@
       implied_by: ["GlobalPrivacyControlForce"],
       base_feature: "none",
     },
     {
       name: "GlobalPrivacyControlForce",
+      status: "stable",
     },
     {
       name: "GraphemeClusterBoundsCheck",
       status: "stable",
     },
